WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.240
Your chance of deciding a presidential election: one in sixty million.

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.390
You're more likely to die in a car crash driving to the polls.

3
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.520
The expected value of your vote is zero point zero zero zero one seven dollars.

4
00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:21.620
The cost of casting it (time, gas, parking) is about fifty dollars.

5
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:27.200
Democracy was designed so that your individual participation is mathematically meaningless.

6
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:34.360
What isn't meaningless: the four point one billion dollars corporations spend annually on lobbying.

7
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:40.260
That math works out just fine. The Founding Fathers were brilliant men.

8
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.720
They anticipated tyranny, corruption, foreign invasion, and mob rule.

9
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:51.560
They did not anticipate Florida. What Democracy Should Look Like versus Reality.

10
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.660
Princeton University analyzed one thousand seven hundred and seventy-nine policy decisions over twenty years.

11
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:04.340
The correlation between what ordinary voters want and what actually happens?

12
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:10.590
Zero. Percent. When economic elites want something? Seventy-eight percent of the time.

13
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.460
You have the same political influence as your houseplant. Slightly less, actually.

14
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.580
Your houseplant produces oxygen, which at least one congressman needs.

15
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:28.620
Your institutions tell the whole story in their names. You have a "Department of Defense" that mainly attacks people.

16
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.880
A "Department of Health" that has not yet produced any observable health.

17
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.780
And a "Department of Justice" that (you can see where this is going).

18
00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:44.560
Whether zero percent or one hundred percent of voters support a bill, it has the same about thirty percent chance of passing.

19
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:51.860
Democracy is a slot machine where every pull gives the same result but you keep playing because the machine has an American

20
00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:59.960
flag on it. The Mathematics of Political Failure. Concentrated Benefits versus Diffuse Costs.

21
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:05.840
Sugar subsidies cost Americans three point five billion dollars per year.

22
00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:11.600
That's ten dollars per person. Will you spend weeks fighting to save ten dollars?

23
00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:18.180
No. Will sugar companies spend millions to keep their three point five billion dollars?

24
00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:28.500
Yes. Guess who wins? This pattern repeats everywhere: Pharma companies see five hundred billion dollars in concentrated

25
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:34.340
profits. Patients endure one thousand five hundred dollars each in diffuse suffering.

26
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:41.940
The winner is not you. Banks in two thousand and eight received a seven hundred billion dollar bailout (concentrated).

27
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.680
You pay two thousand one hundred dollars in taxes (diffuse).

28
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:57.760
The winner is the ones with yachts. How Money Buys Power. Campaign contributions don't directly buy how politicians vote.

29
00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:03.220
Only one in four studies support that. The system is much more elegant than bribery.

30
00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.140
Bribery is what poor countries do. Rich countries have "campaign finance."

31
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:14.640
But they do something far more effective: they buy which politicians exist in the first place.

32
00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:20.860
Ninety-five percent of House races since two thousand and four were won by the highest spender.

33
00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.780
The average winning House campaign costs two point seven nine million dollars.

34
00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:30.840
The average winning Senate campaign costs twenty-six point five three million dollars.

35
00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:36.520
Why bribe a politician when you can just manufacture one who already agrees with you?

36
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.720
It's like complaining about the menu when you own the restaurant.

37
00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:46.620
For the ones already in office, contributions buy something more subtle than votes: Access.

38
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.720
Your call gets returned, theirs goes to voicemail. Agenda control.

39
00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.760
Your issue gets a hearing, theirs gets buried. Legislative language.

40
00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.880
The exact wording that creates loopholes. Committee positions.

41
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.620
Four hundred and fifty thousand dollars to party committees buys a seat where bills live or die.

42
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:11.400
You don't buy the vote. You buy the voter, the agenda, and the room where the vote happens.

43
00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:22.300
The Lobbying R.O.I.: eighteen to one Returns. The most profitable investment in America isn't stocks, real estate, or crypto.

44
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.140
It's congressmen: Disease advocacy groups spend one hundred million dollars on lobbying.

45
00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.060
They get one point eight billion dollars in increased funding.

46
00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:40.020
The R.O.I. is an eighteen to one ratio (seventeen hundred percent net return).

47
00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:46.080
Meanwhile: Breast cancer has great lobbyists which results in massive funding.

48
00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:51.540
Heart disease kills more people, but having worse lobbyists results in less funding.

49
00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:57.300
Rare pediatric diseases have no lobbyists and therefore receive no funding.

50
00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:04.480
Democracy doesn't allocate resources by who's dying. It allocates by who can afford the best lobbyist.

51
00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.600
If your disease can't hire a P.R. firm, it's not a real disease.

52
00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:14.760
It's just dying. Your Congressman: A Fundraiser Who Occasionally Legislates.

53
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.320
The average congressman spends four hours a day on fundraising.

54
00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.940
The average congressman spends zero hours a day reading bills.

55
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.560
Actual governing is whatever time is left. They don't read what they vote on.

56
00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.600
The Patriot Act was three hundred and forty-two pages, voted on forty-five minutes after release.

57
00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:42.140
That's seven point six pages per minute. Olympic speed-readers can't do that.

58
00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:49.840
The Affordable Care Act was twenty-seven hundred pages, and the famous quote was "we have to pass it to see what's in it".

59
00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:57.180
These people control nuclear weapons. Sleep tight. Congressional Committees: The Menu.

60
00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:05.000
Committees control which bills live or die. Both parties assign fundraising quotas for each seat: raise this much for the

61
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:12.500
party, or lose your assignment. Don't hit your number? Your bills die, your amendments vanish, and your name goes on a literal

62
00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:17.450
"wall of shame". It's like a restaurant where the waiters have to pay for their own sections.

63
00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:24.340
Here's the menu. Want to be House Speaker? Raise twenty to thirty-one million dollars for your party.

64
00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:28.850
Want to chair a powerful committee like Ways and Means or Appropriations?

65
00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.940
That'll cost you about one point two to one point eight million dollars in party fundraising.

66
00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:40.960
Even a basic freshman member owes a six-figure fundraising quota just to keep their seat at the table.

67
00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:49.780
For the House Speaker position, the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, or D.C.C.C., requires thirty-one million

68
00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:57.760
dollars and the National Republican Congressional Committee, or N.R.C.C., requires twenty million dollars because the Speaker

69
00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:05.860
controls the entire agenda. For the Ways and Means Chair, where all tax laws start, Democrats must raise one point eight

70
00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:09.780
million dollars and Republicans must raise one point two million dollars.

71
00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:17.020
For the Appropriations Chair, which controls the one point seven trillion dollar budget, Democrats must raise one point

72
00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:20.480
eight million dollars and Republicans must raise one point two million dollars.

73
00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:28.100
For Energy and Commerce, covering healthcare, energy, and telecommunications, Democrats must raise one point eight million

74
00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:28.100
dollars and Republicans must raise one point two million dollars.

75
00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:28.260
A Regular Committee seat costs between five hundred thousand and one million dollars for Democrats and eight hundred and

76
00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:32.260
seventy-five thousand dollars for Republicans, which still beats irrelevance.

77
00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:39.280
A Basic Member owes at least one hundred and fifty thousand dollars as the entry fee to play.

78
00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:43.320
This is why congressmen spend four hours a day fundraising.

79
00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:50.600
They're not campaigning. They're paying rent. The people writing your healthcare laws literally bought their positions from

80
00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:55.780
the industries those laws regulate. On your planet, this is called "the system working."

81
00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:04.620
Public Choice Theory: The Nobel Prize for Cynicism. In nineteen eighty-six, James Buchanan won the Nobel Prize in Economics

82
00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:09.240
for formally proving what anyone who has ever been to the D.M.V.

83
00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:14.200
already knew: People don't become angels when you give them a government I.D.

84
00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:22.720
badge. This is Public Choice Theory: bureaucrats, politicians, and regulators are just as selfish as everyone else.

85
00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:27.840
The only difference is that when a corporation is greedy, you can stop buying their product.

86
00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:32.720
When a bureaucracy is greedy, they can arrest you for not buying their product.

87
00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:42.640
What They Actually Maximize. The N.I.H. should maximize cures, but actually maximizes grants awarded, resulting in no cures.

88
00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:50.340
The F.D.A. should maximize lives saved, but actually maximizes avoiding lawsuits, resulting in deadly delays.

89
00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:58.060
Congress should maximize voter welfare, but actually maximizes campaign donations, resulting in corporate welfare.

90
00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:06.850
Pharma should maximize health outcomes, but actually maximizes recurring revenue, meaning chronic disease is preferred.

91
00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:14.360
Defense contractors should maximize national security, but actually maximize government contracts, resulting in endless

92
00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:19.170
wars. Nobody in the system is incentivized to cure disease.

93
00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:27.160
Or end war. You could elect five hundred and thirty-five clones of Gandhi and within two years they'd be taking pharma money.

94
00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:33.700
The system digests idealists like a stomach digests food. It doesn't matter what you put in.

95
00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:45.840
You get the same output. The Libertarian Paradox. Libertarians say government fails because government is government.

96
00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:49.620
Socialists say government fails because it needs more government.

97
00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:50.340
Public Choice Theory says government fails because the people running it are people, and people are terrible.

98
00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:50.680
The solution is to stop expecting people to be good and design systems where being selfish accidentally produces good outcomes,

99
00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.650
like capitalism, but for not dying. Regulatory Capture: How Industries Write Their Own Rules.

100
00:09:53.680 --> 00:10:37.040
There are thirteen thousand seven hundred registered lobbyists in Washington.

101
00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:41.680
Half of them used to work in the government they're now bribing, which on your planet is called "a career path" rather than

102
00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:42.420
"corruption." In twenty twenty-three, corporations spent four point one billion dollars on lobbying.

103
00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:47.440
Divide that by five hundred and thirty-five members of Congress and you get seven point seven million dollars in professional

104
00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:50.770
persuasion surrounding each congressman who earns less than a mid-level dentist.

105
00:10:55.420 --> 00:12:05.340
The mystery is not that they're corrupt. The mystery is that any of them aren't.

106
00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:09.260
Citizens United made corporations people who can donate unlimited money through Super PACs.

107
00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:19.520
Corporations are people now, which is odd because you can't imprison a corporation, give it chemotherapy, or make it feel

108
00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:19.780
shame. Pharmaceutical companies write their own regulations.

109
00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:21.300
The Medicare Modernization Act banned the government from negotiating drug prices.

110
00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:22.620
That's why insulin that costs six dollars to make costs you three hundred dollars.

111
00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:23.880
A four thousand nine hundred percent markup. Your street-corner drug dealers find this excessive, and they don't even have

112
00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:24.280
shareholders. The military-industrial complex distributes operations across forty-two states so that cutting any program

113
00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:24.380
threatens local jobs in enough congressional districts to make it impossible.

114
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
impossible. It's a tumor that grew its own blood supply. The Pentagon has never passed an audit but somehow knows it needs

115
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
more money (they can't tell you where the last trillion went, but they're very confident about needing the next one).

116
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
Pharma employs three lobbyists for every member of Congress.

117
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
The F.D.A. gets seventy-five percent of its drug review budget from the companies it regulates.

118
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
For details, see F.D.A.: Unsafe and Ineffective. The system maintains itself with the elegant efficiency of a parasite that

119
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
keeps its host alive just enough to keep feeding. Cures are a one-time payment; treatments are recurring revenue.

120
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
Approving a bad drug ends careers; delaying a good one ends lives (but not careers, and careers are what matter to people

121
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
with careers). Everyone who matters wins. Everyone who's dying loses.

122
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
The system works perfectly. It's just optimized for the wrong thing.

123
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
The Solution They Don't Want. The whole system runs on one trick: each American loses about twelve dollars a year to lobbying

124
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
(not worth a phone call). Each corporation gains one hundred million dollars (worth a thousand phone calls, a lobbyist army,

125
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
and a senator's vacation home). But what if curing disease was more profitable than treating it?

126
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
What if peace paid better than war? That's what a one percent treaty does.

127
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
It doesn't fight the corruption machine. It feeds it different inputs.

128
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
You're going to out-bribe the bribers with math. P. S. - If this chapter depressed you, good.

129
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
Depression is the correct response to your political system.

130
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
But remember: the same corruption that lets corporations buy politicians also lets you buy them back.

131
00:12:24.524 --> 00:12:24.524
Politicians don't have principles. They have prices. And prices, unlike principles, can be negotiated.
